<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Financial Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1898</span> · Also known as International Financial Control</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Financial Commission was a body of representatives of the great powers set up in 1898 to supervise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s public finances. Imposed after Greece's state bankruptcy of 1893 and its defeat in the Greco-Turkish War of 1897, it collected certain state revenues, such as monopolies and customs, to pay the war indemnity to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and service foreign loans. It remained a lasting symbol of foreign control over the Greek state's finances.</p></div>
